From 3ba59116bbdf43df8d1f764f14ff56cbc8c918ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Roch=C3=A9=20Compaan?= <roche@upfrontsoftware.co.za>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:02:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 18/18] qwen35: reduce dense Q6_K decode through __dp4a too

Q6_K is 27% of the bytes this checkpoint streams per token and was still on the
float path. Its 210-byte block stride leaves most blocks off a 4-byte boundary,
so the quantised bytes are assembled from byte loads rather than cast to int32,
and the -32 bias folds into the byte arithmetic so the integer sum stays exact
with no bsums correction.

Measured: 29.62 -> 30.41 t/s generation on the 27B with weights resident. Q6_K was
27% of the bytes for 0.8 t/s, which says the decode loop is now close to
memory-bound rather than arithmetic-bound.

The quant suite had no Q4_K case at all, so the dp4a kernel from 0016 was
uncovered; it now covers both types. Because the dp4a paths quantise activations
to q8_K, they are compared against the magnitude of the accumulation with a 2e-2
bound, while the exact float matrix path keeps 5e-5: a dot product that cancels
to near zero otherwise fails a result-relative bound for the error of its own
terms.
